1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
These tools are high-impact, cutting-edge, and big. Really big.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Their challenge? Build hundreds of feet straight up.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:15,000
The indispensable tools of the Hi-Rise, taking us from dirt lots to soaring towers.

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Super Tools. Skyscrapers. Next on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Building a skyscraper is an extraordinary feat of human ingenuity.

6
00:00:40,001 --> 00:00:45,280
Exposing millions of pounds of concrete and steel to the twin enemy forces of wind and

7
00:00:45,281 --> 00:00:46,280
gravity.

8
00:00:46,281 --> 00:00:55,120
From New York, from Chicago, and from Las Vegas, we're gonna get an unusual perspective

9
00:00:55,121 --> 00:01:02,120
on how a skyscraper gets built by going behind the scenes with the tools that make it possible.

10
00:01:02,121 --> 00:01:16,121
The tower crane, the impact wrench, the power trowel, the total station, and the foundation

11
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:20,920
drill rig.

12
00:01:20,921 --> 00:01:27,160
The construction industry builds at a very rapid pace now, and without these tools you

13
00:01:27,161 --> 00:01:32,000
could never maintain the pace that is expected by ownership.

14
00:01:32,001 --> 00:01:36,100
The first step in building any skyscraper, making the foundation.

15
00:01:36,101 --> 00:01:40,540
To do that, holes are dug and then filled with concrete.

16
00:01:40,541 --> 00:01:43,580
These shafts will support the enormous weight of the building.

17
00:01:43,581 --> 00:01:47,400
The foundation is the key element.

18
00:01:47,401 --> 00:01:50,900
If it's not correct, the building will sink or the building will lean.

19
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:56,500
To prepare the support structure, a key tool is deployed.

20
00:01:57,500 --> 00:01:59,200
The Foundation Drill Rig.

21
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:04,000
And here in Chicago, the rig is being put to the test.

22
00:02:04,001 --> 00:02:07,000
We are working on the Trump International Hotel and Tower.

23
00:02:07,001 --> 00:02:09,800
We are installing the deep foundations for the building.

24
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:13,000
The building is going to rise to 92 stories.

25
00:02:13,001 --> 00:02:17,300
That translates into a weight of 720 million pounds.

26
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:23,400
But the soil in Chicago is made of clay, which can't support that kind of weight.

27
00:02:23,401 --> 00:02:27,400
So the building will have to rest on the layer of bedrock below the clay.

28
00:02:27,401 --> 00:02:32,400
And that means digging foundation shafts 170 feet deep.

29
00:02:32,401 --> 00:02:37,400
Today, the crew has to finish the last 30 feet of a hole they began two days ago.

30
00:02:37,401 --> 00:02:41,400
They're using a drill bit called an earth auger.

31
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:52,400
We use this to drill down to the top of rock actually.

32
00:02:52,401 --> 00:02:56,400
And as you can see underneath the auger, there's a row of earth teeth.

33
00:02:56,401 --> 00:03:00,400
They're flat teeth and that's what digs the clay out.

34
00:03:00,401 --> 00:03:05,400
When the auger is full, then they hoist the whole auger out of the ground and they spin the clay off.

35
00:03:05,401 --> 00:03:08,400
Dricker extract over here.

36
00:03:08,401 --> 00:03:13,400
But the earth auger is just one type of interchangeable drill bit they're using here.

37
00:03:13,401 --> 00:03:20,400
And those bits are just one of the components that make up the foundation drill rig.

38
00:03:20,401 --> 00:03:26,400
First, a 120-foot crane holds the whole apparatus in place.

39
00:03:26,401 --> 00:03:32,400
A diesel engine provides the turning power for what's known as a Kelly bar.

40
00:03:32,401 --> 00:03:45,400
All the tools have heads that fit into the Kelly Bar, and whether it's an earth auger,

41
00:03:45,401 --> 00:03:52,400
if it's a rock auger, a core bucket, the Kelly Bar holds all the tools in place and allows

42
00:03:52,401 --> 00:03:55,400
the excavations to happen.

43
00:03:55,401 --> 00:04:01,400
He's reversing the Kelly Bar right now.

44
00:04:01,401 --> 00:04:05,480
Digging each foundation shaft takes between three to five days.

45
00:04:05,481 --> 00:04:06,900
He's got it.

46
00:04:06,901 --> 00:04:10,200
And there are 57 needed for just this building.

47
00:04:10,201 --> 00:04:15,020
It's a pace and scale that was unthinkable at the beginning of the skyscraper era in

48
00:04:15,021 --> 00:04:17,520
the 1880s.

49
00:04:17,521 --> 00:04:21,520
Back then, workers dug foundations by hand with shovels and pickaxes.

50
00:04:21,521 --> 00:04:24,640
A process called hand mucking.

51
00:04:24,641 --> 00:04:27,180
It was very inefficient.

52
00:04:27,181 --> 00:04:31,020
When they're doing hand mucking, it's a slow process, slow and tedious.

53
00:04:31,021 --> 00:04:38,020
It took workers almost a year to dig the foundation shafts for one of New York's earliest skyscrapers,

54
00:04:38,021 --> 00:04:42,020
the 34-story tall municipal building begun in 1909.

55
00:04:42,021 --> 00:04:49,020
By 1913, skyscrapers like the Woolworth building were reaching heights of over 50 stories.

56
00:04:49,021 --> 00:04:55,020
With buildings getting taller and heavier, foundations needed to go deeper.

57
00:04:55,021 --> 00:04:58,020
Manual digging was just not up to the task.

58
00:04:58,021 --> 00:05:03,780
So over the next decade, various mechanical digging devices began to be used on foundations.

59
00:05:04,980 --> 00:05:11,780
But a breakthrough came in Texas, where water well excavator Ed Duderstadt would find a new use for his drilling equipment.

60
00:05:12,140 --> 00:05:18,260
In 1922, my grandfather was drilling water wells on a ranch in a part of the Bexar County that's now San Antonio.

61
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:23,160
While he was working there, the owner was in the process of developing plans to build a new home.

62
00:05:23,161 --> 00:05:26,720
The engineer that was designing that home was Willard Simpson.

63
00:05:26,721 --> 00:05:32,500
Simpson wanted to build a solid foundation for the house, but he was confronted by unstable

64
00:05:32,501 --> 00:05:34,420
soil in San Antonio.

65
00:05:34,421 --> 00:05:39,100
Willard had been toying with the idea of doing drilled shaft foundations instead of what

66
00:05:39,101 --> 00:05:46,880
at that time would have been hand-dug spread footings.

67
00:05:46,881 --> 00:05:52,000
To drill the shafts, Duderstadt and Simpson teamed up and repurposed a water well digging

68
00:05:52,001 --> 00:05:53,000
rig.

69
00:05:53,001 --> 00:05:55,120
The machine was fairly primitive.

70
00:05:55,121 --> 00:05:57,520
In fact, it was powered by horses or mules.

71
00:05:58,420 --> 00:06:02,280
The top part here is called the turntable, and it rotated.

72
00:06:02,840 --> 00:06:07,980
The operator rode on the turntable, and so he was going around in a circle the entire time.

73
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:14,140
As the turntable rotated, it turned the kelly bar, twisting the auger deep into the earth.

74
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:19,060
When the auger's full of dirt, then the winch was used to pull the auger out of the hole,

75
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:21,780
and the dirt was just deposited out here in front of it.

76
00:06:21,781 --> 00:06:25,780
Compared to the roughly two feet an hour it would have taken to dig by hand,

77
00:06:25,781 --> 00:06:29,780
the drill rig was able to dig at a rate of 10 feet an hour.

78
00:06:29,781 --> 00:06:32,780
And those were the first drilled piers done in San Antonio,

79
00:06:32,781 --> 00:06:34,780
and we believe in possibly in the nation.

80
00:06:34,781 --> 00:06:40,780
With this early success, the Foundation drill rig industry was born.

81
00:06:40,781 --> 00:06:46,780
In the 1950s, new drill bits were introduced which were designed specifically for local soil conditions.

82
00:06:46,781 --> 00:06:53,780
The bucket auger came from California because it could scoop out the soft, sandy soil.

83
00:06:53,781 --> 00:06:59,360
And the spiral auger developed in Texas because this type of auger could more easily slice

84
00:06:59,361 --> 00:07:02,780
through the harder clay soil in the Lone Star State.

85
00:07:02,781 --> 00:07:09,300
A decade later, super hard tungsten carbine began to be used on the drill bits.

86
00:07:09,301 --> 00:07:14,440
But the elusive goal of pulverizing bedrock would have to wait until the 1970s at the

87
00:07:14,441 --> 00:07:17,480
Trans-Alaskan Oil Pipeline.

88
00:07:17,481 --> 00:07:20,720
For that project, the percussion hammer was developed.

89
00:07:20,721 --> 00:07:24,760
The percussion hammer would readily drill through the permafrost

90
00:07:24,761 --> 00:07:29,940
and be able to drill any rock formations that were encountered.

91
00:07:29,941 --> 00:07:32,360
But the tool was soon found to be strong enough

92
00:07:32,361 --> 00:07:35,380
that it could plow through bedrock itself.

93
00:07:35,381 --> 00:07:38,080
After the Alaska Pipeline project,

94
00:07:38,081 --> 00:07:41,560
they were carried on into the foundation business.

95
00:07:41,561 --> 00:07:46,440
Beginning in the 1980s, the percussion hammer was improved by mounting multiple hammering

96
00:07:46,441 --> 00:07:51,160
heads onto one big drill bit, allowing for quicker drilling.

97
00:07:51,161 --> 00:07:56,600
And these tools remain a mainstay of the industry, including at the worksite in Chicago, where

98
00:07:56,601 --> 00:08:00,740
the percussion hammer is seeing action.

99
00:08:00,741 --> 00:08:06,320
When lowered into the hole, the individual hammers bounce up and down, crushing the bedrock.

100
00:08:06,321 --> 00:08:12,780
The pulverized rock is collected in a basket on top of the tool and dumped.

101
00:08:12,781 --> 00:08:19,080
After the hole is dug, this seven-foot wide, 105-foot long piece of steel casing is inserted,

102
00:08:19,081 --> 00:08:21,080
which also makes use of carbide.

103
00:08:21,081 --> 00:08:27,320
On the seven-foot diameter casing, there's probably about 35 to 40 carbide teeth, and

104
00:08:27,321 --> 00:08:31,700
that allows the casing to be twisted right through the bedrock.

105
00:08:31,701 --> 00:08:40,700
that's left filling up the hole with 85 cubic yards of cement 49 foundation shafts down only

106
00:08:40,701 --> 00:08:48,020
eight to go but that's only the first step it'll take many months and many more tools to build this

107
00:08:48,021 --> 00:08:57,020
skyscraper the biggest foundation drill rig in the world is called big stand it can drill monster

108
00:08:57,021 --> 00:09:01,340
holes 30 feet in diameter, double the size of the average living room.

109
00:09:01,960 --> 00:09:03,720
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

110
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:09,600
We now return to Super Tools, skyscrapers on Modern Marvels.

111
00:09:12,280 --> 00:09:16,840
After laying the foundation for the skyscraper, the next stage, building up.

112
00:09:17,800 --> 00:09:22,420
And that means lifting thousands of pounds of materials hundreds of feet into the air.

113
00:09:22,421 --> 00:09:27,500
And it's only possible with the tower crane.

114
00:09:34,160 --> 00:09:37,300
To see one in action, we're off to Las Vegas.

115
00:09:38,180 --> 00:09:40,900
Okay, we got a famous bucket on up there and flip it over.

116
00:09:41,720 --> 00:09:46,480
It's like the bat in baseball.

117
00:09:46,740 --> 00:09:48,480
Without the bat, you don't play the game.

118
00:09:49,680 --> 00:09:50,400
All right, we got it.

119
00:09:50,401 --> 00:09:52,400
The tower crane is like the center.

120
00:09:52,401 --> 00:09:55,400
You're not going to win a championship without a top-notch center.

121
00:09:55,401 --> 00:09:57,400
Got about 20 feet.

122
00:09:57,401 --> 00:09:59,400
The tower crane is like Shaquille O'Neal.

123
00:09:59,401 --> 00:10:01,400
It's a little taller, maybe.

124
00:10:01,401 --> 00:10:05,400
Without a tower crane, you wouldn't be building this building in 18 months.

125
00:10:05,401 --> 00:10:08,400
You'd be building it in about four years.

126
00:10:08,401 --> 00:10:14,400
This new 30-story tall hotel tower for the Palms Casino is nearing completion.

127
00:10:14,401 --> 00:10:21,400
But the tower crane still has a lot of lifting to do.

128
00:10:21,401 --> 00:10:30,400
Steel, concrete, scaffolding, windows, and even other tools.

129
00:10:30,401 --> 00:10:34,400
Around the construction site, the tower crane sets the pace.

130
00:10:34,401 --> 00:10:39,400
It's one of the few things where if it doesn't work, if it doesn't run, or the operator's not there,

131
00:10:39,401 --> 00:10:42,400
there's nothing that can be done on the job.

132
00:10:42,401 --> 00:10:47,820
The vertical section of the tower crane is called the mast.

133
00:10:47,821 --> 00:10:50,620
The horizontal piece at the top has two parts.

134
00:10:50,621 --> 00:10:56,160
The longer one in front is the jib, and the shorter one at the back is the machine arm.

135
00:10:56,161 --> 00:11:01,420
Located in the jib is a track-mounted trolley that pulls the load in and out.

136
00:11:01,421 --> 00:11:07,560
Up to 1,800 feet of twisted steel cable, called a load line, does the lifting.

137
00:11:07,561 --> 00:11:13,240
It runs from a cable drum in the machine arm, through the trolley, and down to the ground.

138
00:11:13,241 --> 00:11:19,760
To hoist a load, the crane must lift, trolley, and rotate all at the same time.

139
00:11:19,761 --> 00:11:24,160
During actual lifting, a key element of the crane comes into play.

140
00:11:24,161 --> 00:11:25,960
The counterweights.

141
00:11:25,961 --> 00:11:31,320
Huge slabs of concrete encased in metal that weigh 8,000 pounds each.

142
00:11:31,321 --> 00:11:33,800
The tower crane is basically a big balancing act.

143
00:11:33,801 --> 00:11:38,600
When you pick up loads with the jib, you want your counterweights to pull the crane back into balance.

144
00:11:39,020 --> 00:11:47,080
It can pick up 22 tons out to 110 feet, but it can also take 8,000 pounds out to 230 feet.

145
00:11:48,100 --> 00:11:51,300
It's essential that these careful calculations are followed.

146
00:11:51,960 --> 00:11:54,520
If not, there can be fatal consequences.

147
00:11:54,521 --> 00:12:02,520
In 1999, a super-sized crane called Big Blue was being used to construct Miller Park, the

148
00:12:02,521 --> 00:12:05,240
new home field for the Milwaukee Brewers.

149
00:12:05,241 --> 00:12:09,520
During the building of the roof, the crane lifted a 450-ton metal piece.

150
00:12:09,521 --> 00:12:12,520
What the hell is going on here?

151
00:12:12,521 --> 00:12:16,520
But the load began to sway when the wind suddenly picked up.

152
00:12:16,521 --> 00:12:21,520
Watch it, watch it!

153
00:12:21,521 --> 00:12:33,060
Three construction workers lost their lives in the tragedy.

154
00:12:34,520 --> 00:12:38,980
There's always danger, but there's guidelines, OSHA regulations, federal standards.

155
00:12:39,580 --> 00:12:43,220
When the wind's above 42 mile an hour sustained, we have to shut the crane down.

156
00:12:43,540 --> 00:12:46,360
There's lightning within three to five miles, we have to shut the crane down.

157
00:12:47,780 --> 00:12:50,600
No one knows the risks better than Eugene Clinton.

158
00:12:51,520 --> 00:12:57,120
He's one of the operators of the tower crane being used at the Palms Casino in Las Vegas.

159
00:12:57,121 --> 00:13:01,520
It takes probably a good 15 years of apprenticeship and running cranes on the ground before they

160
00:13:01,521 --> 00:13:03,640
even let you in the seat of one of these tower cranes.

161
00:13:03,641 --> 00:13:07,400
So I'm 27 years into it now.

162
00:13:07,401 --> 00:13:14,660
But before Eugene can put his skills to use each day, he's got to climb all the way up

163
00:13:14,661 --> 00:13:16,660
to the cab.

164
00:13:16,661 --> 00:13:22,660
490 foot climb in the heat of August in Las Vegas in the desert.

165
00:13:22,661 --> 00:13:24,660
They're taking out of them man.

166
00:13:24,661 --> 00:13:26,660
This is halfway.

167
00:13:26,661 --> 00:13:28,660
Yeehaw.

168
00:13:28,661 --> 00:13:32,660
It'll take me about 45 minutes in this heat to get up there to the top.

169
00:13:32,661 --> 00:13:34,660
So we'll take a nice slow climb.

170
00:13:38,660 --> 00:13:40,660
Well, we made it.

171
00:13:40,661 --> 00:13:44,660
Now that the climb's over, we got an easy day.

172
00:13:44,661 --> 00:13:47,420
Bob's gone home, you got Big Gene in the seat.

173
00:13:49,560 --> 00:13:56,000
Big Gene's first job today, lifting this 20,000-pound bucket to the roof 30 floors up.

174
00:13:57,500 --> 00:14:00,880
Hello, Las Vegas. Away we go.

175
00:14:01,540 --> 00:14:03,500
Each lift is called a pick.

176
00:14:03,840 --> 00:14:06,600
Okay, we got a big miss bucket on up there and flip it over.

177
00:14:07,020 --> 00:14:12,180
All the picks done by the crane are pre-planned prior to them being done.

178
00:14:12,600 --> 00:14:13,900
It's not spur of the moment.

179
00:14:14,660 --> 00:14:18,440
A signalman directs the crane's hook and then clamps it onto the bucket.

180
00:14:19,160 --> 00:14:20,160
It's all yours. Take it up.

181
00:14:20,900 --> 00:14:26,760
Then Eugene engages the lift lever, and the 10-ton bucket starts its 400-foot climb.

182
00:14:27,400 --> 00:14:28,100
Nice and easy.

183
00:14:28,280 --> 00:14:30,500
When the load has cleared the top of the building,

184
00:14:30,780 --> 00:14:31,500
All right, swing it.

185
00:14:31,720 --> 00:14:35,180
Eugene swivels the crane, bringing the bucket over the roof.

186
00:14:35,740 --> 00:14:38,000
Total time for this pick? Four minutes.

187
00:14:38,520 --> 00:14:41,840
A monumental achievement that's the state of the art in lifting,

188
00:14:41,841 --> 00:14:43,860
but it wasn't always this fast.

189
00:14:44,660 --> 00:14:54,660
Although manual cranes have been in use since the Romans, it wasn't until 1839 that the first steam-powered crane began to change the construction business.

190
00:14:54,661 --> 00:15:01,660
The brainchild of inventor William Smith Otis, it was called the Crane Excavator and designed to dig.

191
00:15:01,661 --> 00:15:05,660
It had a large shovel attached to a system of winches and pulleys.

192
00:15:05,661 --> 00:15:13,660
But the steam-powered crane soon lost its shovel and gained a hook, making it possible to lift ever greater loads to ever greater heights.

193
00:15:13,661 --> 00:15:14,361
heights.

194
00:15:14,362 --> 00:15:19,180
There was a direct correlation between the development of cranes and barracks and the

195
00:15:19,181 --> 00:15:23,380
size of all types of construction projects that could be undertaken, especially skyscrapers.

196
00:15:23,381 --> 00:15:29,200
But the jibs on the early cranes were raised and lowered from a pivot at ground level,

197
00:15:29,201 --> 00:15:31,972
limiting their reach around building sites.

198
00:15:31,973 --> 00:15:35,260
So the next step forward was to make cranes mobile,

199
00:15:35,261 --> 00:15:41,800
first putting them on rails and then by the early decades of the 20th century on treads or wheels.

200
00:15:43,660 --> 00:15:49,700
Well this is a 1926 link belt crane. It's pretty typical of the type of cranes

201
00:15:49,701 --> 00:15:59,740
that were built in the 1920s. The engine had to be hand cranked to start. If it

202
00:15:59,741 --> 00:16:03,700
doesn't start on three or four cranks you're pretty well exhausted and you

203
00:16:03,701 --> 00:16:07,600
have to get your relief in there to crank it some more. But hand cranking

204
00:16:07,601 --> 00:16:12,440
wasn't this cranes only limitation. Well this crane weighs about 30 tons and as

205
00:16:12,441 --> 00:16:16,460
typical in those days that could only lift about half its weight where today's

206
00:16:16,461 --> 00:16:22,460
cranes would lift typically its own weight. The tower crane, like the one

207
00:16:22,461 --> 00:16:28,960
being used in Vegas, wasn't introduced until 1949. Perfected by German engineer

208
00:16:28,961 --> 00:16:33,680
Hans Liebherr, these new cranes had a horizontal jib at the top, greatly

209
00:16:33,681 --> 00:16:37,400
enhancing their usefulness on construction sites. What made it

210
00:16:37,401 --> 00:16:42,120
different is that this is a crane that could lift materials from the ground and

211
00:16:42,121 --> 00:16:45,960
and hoist it and then swing over the new structure

212
00:16:45,961 --> 00:16:51,000
and place the material on the structure back in where it needed to be used.

213
00:16:51,001 --> 00:16:53,240
And that made a difference.

214
00:16:53,241 --> 00:16:57,640
Anything else had only been delivering materials to the edge of the structures

215
00:16:57,641 --> 00:17:01,240
and carried across the deck by manpower.

216
00:17:01,241 --> 00:17:04,280
Liebherr's tower cranes were hugely successful,

217
00:17:04,281 --> 00:17:08,440
and by the 1950s they dominated construction sites across Europe.

218
00:17:08,441 --> 00:17:14,460
In Dusseldorf, I saw a crane that was reaching up over 300 feet in the air.

219
00:17:15,120 --> 00:17:22,380
And this was the time before we ever had anything over 200 feet in ground-mounted cranes in the United States.

220
00:17:22,800 --> 00:17:26,880
Ben was so impressed that he brought the first tower crane to the United States

221
00:17:26,881 --> 00:17:31,420
for his next construction project, the Sheridan Motor Inn in Binghamton, New York.

222
00:17:31,920 --> 00:17:34,940
But not everyone was as enthusiastic as he was.

223
00:17:34,941 --> 00:17:38,720
The first crane that we brought over, well, it was a tough flight.

224
00:17:38,721 --> 00:17:40,560
And they said, what are you talking about?

225
00:17:40,640 --> 00:17:43,960
I said, my daddy did it this way, and I'm going to continue to do it this way.

226
00:17:44,020 --> 00:17:49,180
And they didn't want to accept the fact that there was a different way that you could build a building.

227
00:17:49,780 --> 00:17:54,740
But with its initial use in Binghamton, and then for the Pittsburgh Hilton Hotel in 1959,

228
00:17:55,260 --> 00:17:57,700
the Tower Crane had conquered America.

229
00:17:59,040 --> 00:18:03,140
And the clincher was locating the operator in a cab hundreds of feet up,

230
00:18:03,141 --> 00:18:07,760
because previously operators had been near the ground and couldn't see very much.

231
00:18:09,040 --> 00:18:13,980
We have always felt that's very important because the operator looking at what he's doing,

232
00:18:14,120 --> 00:18:17,580
both on the ground and where he's placing it on the deck,

233
00:18:17,780 --> 00:18:22,780
is certainly a great safety advantage, but it's certainly a productive advantage.

234
00:18:23,520 --> 00:18:27,160
Tower cranes have gotten much taller and stronger over the decades,

235
00:18:27,520 --> 00:18:29,900
but the biggest improvement, electronics.

236
00:18:29,901 --> 00:18:34,140
I've been working with tower cranes for 24 years now.

237
00:18:34,141 --> 00:18:36,960
When I started, there wasn't no electronics in the cranes.

238
00:18:36,961 --> 00:18:39,940
They've improved dramatically what the cranes can do.

239
00:18:39,941 --> 00:18:45,660
To combat against disastrous accidents, tower cranes now have what's called load limiters,

240
00:18:45,661 --> 00:18:50,260
computerized equipment that measures how much a load weighs, determines its distance from

241
00:18:50,261 --> 00:18:53,700
the mast, and calculates whether the crane can handle the pick.

242
00:18:53,701 --> 00:18:59,660
These are my fault indicators, letting me know these lights light up, if there's a problem,

243
00:18:59,661 --> 00:19:00,820
There's a little siren here.

244
00:19:00,920 --> 00:19:01,880
The alarm goes off.

245
00:19:04,960 --> 00:19:10,240
During today's shift, Eugene and his crane lifted a total of 21,000 pounds of material.

246
00:19:10,880 --> 00:19:13,720
I built about a half a dozen of these high-rise buildings around here,

247
00:19:13,800 --> 00:19:15,860
including Mandalay Bay and Caesars and Bellagio.

248
00:19:16,100 --> 00:19:18,300
So it's fun to look back and see the work you've completed.

249
00:19:18,700 --> 00:19:19,560
It's a great job.

250
00:19:19,561 --> 00:19:22,200
And, hey, living in Las Vegas, what do you say, baby?

251
00:19:23,720 --> 00:19:27,720
The largest tower crane in the world is the Kroll K-10,000.

252
00:19:27,721 --> 00:19:32,620
it can lift more than 260 tons and costs over $9 million.

253
00:19:33,400 --> 00:19:35,560
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

254
00:19:36,880 --> 00:19:41,720
We now return to Super Tools, skyscrapers on Modern Marvels.

255
00:19:47,560 --> 00:19:51,640
The next stage in building a skyscraper, connecting everything together.

256
00:19:52,400 --> 00:19:54,440
And for that, you need bolts.

257
00:19:55,120 --> 00:19:56,280
Lots of bolts.

258
00:19:57,720 --> 00:20:03,420
And the only way they're getting tightened is with the impact wrench.

259
00:20:11,360 --> 00:20:14,760
At the site of the new headquarters for the New York Times in Manhattan,

260
00:20:15,240 --> 00:20:17,380
the impact wrench is being put through its paces.

261
00:20:19,260 --> 00:20:22,000
We're in the corner of 41st Street and 8th Avenue in Manhattan.

262
00:20:23,060 --> 00:20:27,260
We're standing on the 18th floor right now, and we're erecting the 19th and 20th floor.

263
00:20:27,720 --> 00:20:32,760
The building's eventually going to go to the 52-4s with a 350-foot mast on top of it.

264
00:20:33,400 --> 00:20:36,280
The whole project will weigh about 150 million pounds,

265
00:20:36,480 --> 00:20:39,700
but just the structural steel will be about a third of that 50 million pounds.

266
00:20:40,960 --> 00:20:44,600
Binding that 50 million pounds together is serious work.

267
00:20:44,980 --> 00:20:47,440
There are over 200,000 bolts going into this building.

268
00:20:48,220 --> 00:20:50,620
You would never be able to get these bolts tight by hand.

269
00:20:51,820 --> 00:20:54,680
Then you use the impact wrench to tighten everything up.

270
00:20:54,681 --> 00:21:01,080
Gun it up. Pull it up with the gun and tighten it.

271
00:21:01,081 --> 00:21:05,580
Tightening hundreds of bolts during an eight-hour shift requires a lot of elbow grease.

272
00:21:05,581 --> 00:21:09,680
You know, it's pretty heavy to begin with and a lot of vibration.

273
00:21:09,681 --> 00:21:12,980
It depends on you got to get yourself in the right position to use the gun.

274
00:21:12,981 --> 00:21:15,480
That's a big part of it.

275
00:21:15,481 --> 00:21:19,480
The impact wrench's hidden force? Torque.

276
00:21:19,481 --> 00:21:23,780
You can stumble all over yourself trying to explain what torque is.

277
00:21:23,781 --> 00:21:26,400
What is the tightness of the, how tight the bolt is?

278
00:21:27,420 --> 00:21:28,420
That's the torque.

279
00:21:29,120 --> 00:21:30,240
I'm an iron worker.

280
00:21:31,160 --> 00:21:31,960
Talk to an engineer.

281
00:21:32,520 --> 00:21:36,840
It's actually a force that turns something, that will rotate something.

282
00:21:37,460 --> 00:21:41,640
When pushing down on a hand wrench, the downward force is the torque.

283
00:21:42,760 --> 00:21:48,040
But a key component of torque is distance, so the longer the handle, the more twisting power.

284
00:21:49,020 --> 00:21:55,060
The impact wrenches that we use on this job site are made to bring the bolts up to 1,500 pounds of torque.

285
00:21:55,400 --> 00:22:01,300
To come to 1,500 foot-pounds of torque, a 150-pound man would need a 10-foot-long wrench.

286
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:05,860
But the pneumatic impact wrench dispenses with the need for a long handle

287
00:22:05,861 --> 00:22:09,160
and puts the power into the hands of the steel erectors.

288
00:22:10,460 --> 00:22:12,000
But they do need air.

289
00:22:14,080 --> 00:22:17,500
Like all pneumatic tools, compressed air is pumped in.

290
00:22:18,040 --> 00:22:23,040
Inside a pneumatic wrench is a hammer, an anvil, and a bit.

291
00:22:23,041 --> 00:22:27,040
The forced air activates the hammer, which causes the anvil to spin,

292
00:22:27,041 --> 00:22:30,040
twisting the bit, screwing the bolt into place.

293
00:22:30,041 --> 00:22:34,040
You can tell when the bolt is tight by the sound of the hammer.

294
00:22:34,041 --> 00:22:39,040
When it kind of double hits, you'll know the bolt is up to torque.

295
00:22:40,040 --> 00:22:43,040
But before the bolts can be torqued up,

296
00:22:43,041 --> 00:22:47,040
the steel girders have to be hoisted into position by what's called the raising gang,

297
00:22:47,041 --> 00:22:51,040
Six ironworkers communicating with the crane operator.

298
00:22:51,041 --> 00:22:58,040
Balancing on top of the structure are two men called connectors who grab the girders and wrestle them into place.

299
00:22:58,041 --> 00:23:04,040
They insert a temporary fastener to hold the new beam in place and move on to the next piece.

300
00:23:04,041 --> 00:23:09,040
The next thing we do is send the bolt to rupture as soon as the steel is erected.

301
00:23:09,041 --> 00:23:12,040
And they stick all the bolts in.

302
00:23:12,041 --> 00:23:14,040
He sticks the bolt through to me.

303
00:23:14,041 --> 00:23:16,040
I put the washer and nut on and tighten it up.

304
00:23:20,040 --> 00:23:24,040
This section of the steel structure presents a problem for Kevin and Tommy.

305
00:23:25,040 --> 00:23:30,040
The beams meet at an angle, so they have to pull out what's called the sidewinder.

306
00:23:32,040 --> 00:23:34,040
It's like a 90-degree angle impact gun.

307
00:23:35,040 --> 00:23:37,040
It gets you into tight spots with that.

308
00:23:38,040 --> 00:23:40,040
That one's fun to hold onto sometimes.

309
00:23:40,041 --> 00:23:44,920
There are 35 bolts for just this one connection that they're working on right now.

310
00:23:45,200 --> 00:23:48,320
But each bolt in a skyscraper has a lot riding on it.

311
00:23:48,700 --> 00:23:52,440
The bolts help spread the weight, the compression, all the way down to the base of the building.

312
00:23:55,240 --> 00:24:00,300
But pneumatic impact wrenches have only been used on skyscrapers since the 1950s.

313
00:24:01,240 --> 00:24:04,740
In fact, before then, they didn't even use bolts.

314
00:24:04,741 --> 00:24:11,160
Instead, everything from standard 15-story skyscrapers to monumental projects like the

315
00:24:11,161 --> 00:24:13,840
Empire State Building used rivets.

316
00:24:13,841 --> 00:24:20,400
A method dating back to the 1800s, rivets were ingeniously simple.

317
00:24:20,401 --> 00:24:26,100
It's just a short shaft of iron with a head on one end.

318
00:24:26,101 --> 00:24:33,260
The heated rivet was slipped through a precast hole in a metal beam and pummeled flat.

319
00:24:33,261 --> 00:24:41,020
The rivet is resting in between the two girders and it keeps them together.

320
00:24:41,880 --> 00:24:42,780
They did it on bridges.

321
00:24:42,900 --> 00:24:46,840
They did it on steel erection for large skyscrapers at the time.

322
00:24:48,420 --> 00:24:54,820
In the late 1800s, Joseph Boyer took riveting to the next stage with his pneumatic riveting hammer.

323
00:24:56,720 --> 00:24:59,620
Inside the metal housing of his invention was a piston.

324
00:25:00,420 --> 00:25:04,660
Pressurized air rapidly pushed the piston back and forth, flattening the rivet.

325
00:25:05,040 --> 00:25:08,980
It was such an important tool. It was something that everybody needed.

326
00:25:09,540 --> 00:25:15,000
And as soon as Boyer got his invented and got it on the market in October of 1898,

327
00:25:15,420 --> 00:25:19,980
it instantly became popular. Once it was on the market, it was being used.

328
00:25:22,180 --> 00:25:25,620
But in the 1950s, rivets ran into an obstacle.

329
00:25:25,621 --> 00:25:31,220
Engineers wanted to be able to measure the strength of a single rivet, but that was a problem.

330
00:25:31,221 --> 00:25:36,020
There wasn't any real satisfactory way of determining how much holding power a rivet had.

331
00:25:36,021 --> 00:25:43,020
There's no science to it and you never know for sure if there might be a flaw in the head or in the shank of the rivet.

332
00:25:43,021 --> 00:25:45,420
So it was felt to be unreliable.

333
00:25:45,421 --> 00:25:50,620
In 1951, a pivotal report sounded the death nail for the rivet.

334
00:25:50,621 --> 00:25:57,620
The Research Council on Riveted and Bolted Structural Joints concluded that bolts had the same holding power as rivets.

335
00:25:57,621 --> 00:26:03,620
And since bolts relied on torque to be tightened, engineers finally had a value they could measure.

336
00:26:03,621 --> 00:26:06,620
So bolts were poised to take over.

337
00:26:06,621 --> 00:26:11,620
In the meantime, a tool had been created which would make tightening bolts a snap.

338
00:26:11,621 --> 00:26:19,620
That tool was the impact wrench, developed in 1939 by a tool manufacturing company named Chicago Pneumatic.

339
00:26:19,621 --> 00:26:33,120
And at the time, there was a requirement for a lot more efficiency in the fastening or building of these heavy metal things, tanks and trucks.

340
00:26:34,260 --> 00:26:42,780
But it wasn't until 1957, when it was used in the building of the New Manhattan skyscraper, that the tool would earn its place in building construction.

341
00:26:43,160 --> 00:26:49,160
The Tishman Building in New York was one of the first that was used with impact tools to construct the building.

342
00:26:49,620 --> 00:26:55,380
And of course what happened, as opposed to using a rivet gun, which you needed a four-man crew,

343
00:26:55,500 --> 00:26:58,200
you could start to put these buildings together with two people.

344
00:26:59,460 --> 00:27:04,700
But skyscraper construction keeps evolving, so the tools have to evolve right alongside.

345
00:27:05,340 --> 00:27:09,760
I guess somewhere in the 1980s, it became really widespread in the 1990s.

346
00:27:09,820 --> 00:27:12,020
They introduced the TC bolt, attention control bolt.

347
00:27:12,900 --> 00:27:17,500
The novel new bolt has a tip that breaks off automatically when it reaches the right torque.

348
00:27:17,501 --> 00:27:24,500
To screw it in, they use the TC gun.

349
00:27:29,260 --> 00:27:33,900
Tension control gun has two sockets, an inner socket and an outer socket.

350
00:27:33,901 --> 00:27:38,660
One holds the bolt, one turns the nut. When the nut gets to the proper tension,

351
00:27:38,661 --> 00:27:41,620
the tip of the bolt shears off.

352
00:27:41,621 --> 00:27:45,720
The tension control gun are all electrically powered, so they are a lot lighter,

353
00:27:45,721 --> 00:27:46,720
They're a lot easier to handle.

354
00:27:47,260 --> 00:27:51,800
Kevin and Tommy are working on their 160th and last bolt of the day.

355
00:27:52,560 --> 00:27:55,940
And the New York Times building gets closer to its ultimate height.

356
00:27:56,540 --> 00:28:01,160
It gives you a sense of accomplishment when you put the building up, you know.

357
00:28:01,520 --> 00:28:05,900
The great thing about the high-rise is that there's always a different view, you know, when you come up here.

358
00:28:08,980 --> 00:28:13,560
The largest impact wrench in the world is Ingersoll Rand's 599,

359
00:28:13,561 --> 00:28:15,880
named for its weight in pounds.

360
00:28:16,260 --> 00:28:19,220
It's so large it must be lifted by a crane.

361
00:28:19,960 --> 00:28:21,760
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

362
00:28:23,160 --> 00:28:27,720
We now return to Super Tools, skyscrapers on Modern Marvels.

363
00:28:30,920 --> 00:28:33,460
The next step in building a skyscraper,

364
00:28:34,140 --> 00:28:38,360
creating the floors, which means pouring and smoothing a lot of concrete.

365
00:28:38,361 --> 00:28:47,000
The concrete used for the floors on the New Palms Tower in Las Vegas is designed to support

366
00:28:47,001 --> 00:28:50,280
over a million pounds for each of the building's 30 stories.

367
00:28:50,281 --> 00:28:55,760
In our construction of the tower, we have over 30,000 yards of concrete.

368
00:28:55,761 --> 00:28:59,460
Getting all that concrete 30 stories up is the first challenge.

369
00:28:59,461 --> 00:29:05,600
And for that, a state-of-the-art concrete pump will do the lion's share of the lifting

370
00:29:05,601 --> 00:29:08,600
Or more precisely, push it.

371
00:29:16,600 --> 00:29:18,600
On the ground, there's a pipe that runs up the building,

372
00:29:18,601 --> 00:29:21,600
and the concrete gets pumped from the ground to the pump,

373
00:29:21,601 --> 00:29:24,600
and it gets placed by the boom pump.

374
00:29:24,601 --> 00:29:28,600
But even this high-tech boom can't reach every corner of the roof.

375
00:29:28,601 --> 00:29:32,600
So they also have to use an older method around here, the bucket brigade.

376
00:29:32,601 --> 00:29:38,360
They have two buckets on the site that can hold five yards of concrete each.

377
00:29:38,361 --> 00:29:42,880
As one is being filled on the ground, the other is being lifted to the top by the crane.

378
00:29:42,881 --> 00:29:47,660
As they come up here, the labors and the guy on the radio there, he swings it to wherever

379
00:29:47,661 --> 00:29:50,600
you want to position it and he just put on that latch.

380
00:29:50,601 --> 00:29:55,820
It's got a latch that opens up the openings at the bottom and lets the concrete out.

381
00:29:55,821 --> 00:30:00,460
From the moment the concrete arrives on site, it's a race against time and temperature to

382
00:30:00,461 --> 00:30:02,020
trowel each floor.

383
00:30:02,021 --> 00:30:07,680
This process smooths the concrete and squeezes out any moisture so it will set properly.

384
00:30:07,681 --> 00:30:12,620
With the temperatures that we have in Vegas, between the time you place the concrete, you've

385
00:30:12,621 --> 00:30:16,260
probably got a three to four hour window due to the heat.

386
00:30:16,261 --> 00:30:18,260
Bob, five machines?

387
00:30:18,261 --> 00:30:27,960
And to get that much concrete smoothed that quickly, you have to have the power trowel.

388
00:30:27,961 --> 00:30:31,700
and a tool of choice, the power trowel is a tool of necessity.

389
00:30:32,280 --> 00:30:35,080
No construction company that does concrete could do without them.

390
00:30:35,600 --> 00:30:38,700
The power trowel is a hand trowel on steroids.

391
00:30:47,780 --> 00:30:50,680
The power trowel works sort of like a floor buffer,

392
00:30:51,280 --> 00:30:53,400
but this tool has a lever on the handle

393
00:30:53,401 --> 00:30:56,680
that allows the operator to adjust the angle of the blades.

394
00:30:56,681 --> 00:31:00,580
The operator has to be trained on the power trowel.

395
00:31:00,581 --> 00:31:06,580
It has torque to it, so you have to be used to it to be able to control it.

396
00:31:06,581 --> 00:31:09,580
It takes someone with quite a bit of strength to run them.

397
00:31:09,581 --> 00:31:17,080
But even with the use of the power trowel, workers do still need occasionally to get on their knees to use hand trowels.

398
00:31:17,081 --> 00:31:22,280
You still have to do that to get close to bolts and things like that.

399
00:31:22,281 --> 00:31:26,080
So you still do have to use hand tools.

400
00:31:26,081 --> 00:31:33,760
Hand trowels have been pretty successful in their own right, a premier tool of the trade

401
00:31:33,761 --> 00:31:36,080
since ancient times.

402
00:31:36,081 --> 00:31:45,380
In August 2004, a team excavating a 1500-year-old Roman villa found a trowel embedded in concrete.

403
00:31:45,381 --> 00:31:50,020
A simple tool with a flat piece of metal attached to a handle, the trowel would change little

404
00:31:50,021 --> 00:31:54,960
over the centuries.

405
00:31:54,961 --> 00:31:58,260
however, would see lots of improvements.

406
00:31:58,261 --> 00:32:01,420
The Romans used a mixture of limestone, animal fat,

407
00:32:01,421 --> 00:32:05,340
and blood to make their concrete, a method that would not

408
00:32:05,341 --> 00:32:09,720
change until 1824 when British bricklayer Joseph Aspden

409
00:32:09,721 --> 00:32:13,440
invented Portland cement.

410
00:32:13,441 --> 00:32:16,960
His breakthrough idea was to fire finely pulverized limestone

411
00:32:16,961 --> 00:32:20,100
and clay at high temperatures in kilns.

412
00:32:20,101 --> 00:32:23,920
It was a stronger binder so they could create a stronger

413
00:32:23,921 --> 00:32:28,240
material that had more uses in the building trades.

414
00:32:28,241 --> 00:32:37,600
In 1900, legendary inventor Thomas Edison upped the stakes with his rotary kiln.

415
00:32:37,601 --> 00:32:38,600
His twist?

416
00:32:38,601 --> 00:32:39,600
Length.

417
00:32:39,601 --> 00:32:41,640
150 feet long to be exact.

418
00:32:41,641 --> 00:32:46,600
A longer kiln made better quality concrete, faster.

419
00:32:46,601 --> 00:32:53,740
The increased length allowed for a longer duration in the burning zone, which

420
00:32:53,741 --> 00:32:56,980
created a higher quality cement.

421
00:32:56,981 --> 00:32:59,640
Edison used his concrete to construct houses,

422
00:32:59,641 --> 00:33:02,080
and he laid the first mile of concrete road

423
00:33:02,081 --> 00:33:04,980
located near New Village, New Jersey.

424
00:33:04,981 --> 00:33:09,220
But all this concrete still had to be laboriously laid by hand,

425
00:33:09,221 --> 00:33:11,360
and concrete finishers were getting pretty fed up

426
00:33:11,361 --> 00:33:14,500
spending all that time on their knees.

427
00:33:14,501 --> 00:33:17,700
In 1939, a concrete finisher in California

428
00:33:17,701 --> 00:33:21,200
named Marvin Whiteman was determined to kneel no longer.

429
00:33:21,201 --> 00:33:35,201
And so he scavengered parts from a salvaged washing machine for the motor and then built blades to ride on the concrete, added a handle, and you have your first power

430
00:33:36,629 --> 00:33:37,329
trough.

431
00:33:38,040 --> 00:33:41,920
It was his wife's washing machine, a Kenmore brand from Sears and Roebuck.

432
00:33:42,480 --> 00:33:45,420
But Mrs. Whiteman probably didn't mind too much.

433
00:33:46,040 --> 00:33:50,020
Whiteman patented his invention and made millions selling it to construction companies.

434
00:33:51,200 --> 00:33:53,615
At first they were like a crazy wild stallion.

435
00:33:53,616 --> 00:33:57,500
You'd say to yourself, oh my god, this thing is going to get away from me.

436
00:33:57,501 --> 00:34:03,160
On the original high-rises, they didn't like to use them because whenever you would hit something, it would take off on its own.

437
00:34:03,420 --> 00:34:06,620
They would fly off buildings, yeah? Kind of frightening.

438
00:34:07,760 --> 00:34:15,360
And so they developed a clutch system that allows with a simple lever on the handle to start the machine,

439
00:34:15,361 --> 00:34:21,540
get control of the handles, depress the clutch, and it would start the rotation of the blades.

440
00:34:22,140 --> 00:34:27,980
With this kill switch, as it's called, the tool can't move unless the operator's hands are engaging the clutch.

441
00:34:28,340 --> 00:34:30,360
So no more flying power trowels.

442
00:34:33,620 --> 00:34:38,680
The next big advance came in the 1970s, a ride-on power trowel.

443
00:34:38,681 --> 00:34:45,000
Available as either gas or diesel powered, these rideable versions have two sets of spinning

444
00:34:45,001 --> 00:34:49,280
blades and the blades themselves are longer.

445
00:34:49,281 --> 00:34:53,800
They're able to cover four to five times as much concrete as a walk-behind model.

446
